WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.779
CI/CD compromise can turn a development pipeline into an attack path.

2
00:00:04.779 --> 00:00:13.111
Source control permissions, runner environments, deployment keys, artifact repositories, and build scripts all need protection.

3
00:00:13.111 --> 00:00:20.953
Insecure deserialization allows attackers to manipulate serialized input and influence object state or server behavior.

4
00:00:20.953 --> 00:00:27.279
This can lead to control bypass, data tampering, or code execution in vulnerable patterns.

5
00:00:27.279 --> 00:00:34.453
Unsigned code and unverified artifacts make it difficult to prove that deployed software is authentic and unchanged.

6
00:00:34.453 --> 00:00:38.634
Integrity validation should cover the path from source to build to release.

7
00:00:38.634 --> 00:00:44.481
Build pipeline attacks are especially relevant when automation deploys to systems that handle CUI.

8
00:00:44.481 --> 00:00:50.787
Change control, separation of duties, approvals, and monitoring help keep the pipeline trustworthy.

9
00:00:50.787 --> 00:00:54.199
Use code signing and artifact integrity validation.

10
00:00:54.199 --> 00:00:57.821
Secure build pipelines with least privilege and approvals.

11
00:00:57.821 --> 00:01:01.763
Restrict and monitor changes to software and deployment processes.

12
